    "role": "assistant",
    "type": "assistant_message",
    "content": "All 19 checklist items have been tested and recorded. Testing is complete.\n\n**Summary: 17 PASS / 2 FAIL**\n\nThe core to-do functionality is solid — task CRUD, priority/deadline sorting (including correct placement of undated tasks last), active/completed view switching with accurate counts, empty-title validation, prerequisite assignment, blocked-state gating, and both hover tooltips (dependency-chain popup on the dashed SVG connectors and on the blocked-task warning badge) all behaved as specified. Cycle prevention was notably robust, excluding both direct and transitive dependents from the prerequisite picker.\n\nTwo defects were found:\n\n**FT-3 — Blocked tasks are completely uneditable.** The spec requires that a blocked task still allow description and priority changes while locking title and deadline. In practice the edit button carries the HTML `disabled` attribute, and a full DOM scan of the task card turns up only three controls (complete: disabled, edit: disabled, delete: enabled). There is no inline or alternative affordance, so the intended partial-edit capability does not exist at all.\n\n**CS-22 — Reopening a prerequisite creates an unrecoverable invalid state.** After completing \"QA Full Task\" and then reopening its prerequisite \"Schedule dentist appointment,\" the dependent stayed counted and styled as completed (line-through, `data-semtag-state=\"completed\"`, listed only in the completed view) while *simultaneously* rendering the blocked warning. The system neither blocked the reopen nor reverted the dependent to incomplete. This is worse than a cosmetic inconsistency: because blocked tasks have both complete and edit disabled, the user has no way to toggle or edit the task out of this state — the only escape is deletion, which loses the task. This is the more serious of the two findings, and note that it stems from the same design decision behind FT-3, since a working partial-edit path would not rescue it either."
  }
}
